const faqs = [
  {
    q: 'How long does shipping from China to Denmark take?',
    a: 'Allow roughly 32–40 days port-to-port by sea from Shanghai or Ningbo-Zhoushan to Aarhus via the Cape (MEDIUM confidence, planning estimate), plus about 1–2 days inland — versus roughly 20–24 days by China-Europe rail via Hamburg. No verified Danish air transit is published, so request that from your carrier.',
  },
  {
    q: 'How much does shipping from China to Denmark cost?',
    a: 'The published benchmark is the northern-range Rotterdam reference — $1,200–$4,800 per 20ft and $1,600–$6,800 per 40ft (MEDIUM), with LCL at $60–$160 per CBM — while Aarhus-specific figures are not published and must be requested. Rail is roughly 2–3× sea and air is quote-only, so the ~15 CBM LCL/FCL break-even is the first number to pin down.',
  },
  {
    q: 'What is the VAT rate in Denmark?',
    a: 'Denmark applies a single 25% standard VAT rate with no reduced band — 0% applies only to newspapers and periodicals. Import VAT is charged on the duty-inclusive value, so 5% duty plus 25% VAT compounds to ×1.3125, not 30% — a $10,000 CIF shipment at 5% duty lands at $13,125 before destination fees.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Which port should I use when shipping from China to Denmark?',
    a: 'Aarhus (UN/LOCODE DKAAR) is Denmark\u2019s largest container port, handling roughly 70% of Danish container traffic and reached from the North Sea via the Skagerrak/Kattegat, while Copenhagen (DKCPH) serves the Zealand/Øresund capital market and Fredericia (DKFRH) serves Jutland and Funen on the Little Belt — so the port follows the destination, not a single national gate.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Why is Denmark\u2019s currency (DKK) different from Sweden\u2019s (SEK) for landed cost?',
    a: 'Denmark\u2019s krone is pegged to the euro at ±2.25% under ERM II (1 EUR ≈ 7.46 DKK), so the EUR→DKK leg is fixed and only the USD→EUR leg floats — whereas Sweden\u2019s krona floats freely against both. That bounds Denmark\u2019s currency-driven VAT swing to ±2.25% (about ±$59 on a $10,000 CIF at 5% duty), roughly half the exposure of a floating-currency market.',
  },
  {
    q: 'How does Denmark\u2019s landed cost compare with Germany\u2019s and Sweden\u2019s?',
    a: 'On a $10,000 CIF at 5% duty, Denmark\u2019s 25% VAT lands at $13,125 — identical to Sweden\u2019s 25% but $630 above Germany\u2019s 19% ($12,495), a 6-percentage-point gap worth $105 per VAT point at 5% duty. So the Denmark-vs-Sweden choice is decided by currency regime and geography, not by VAT.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Can I ship China to Denmark by rail?',
    a: 'Yes — China-Europe rail runs to Hamburg in roughly 18–22 days, then continues to Denmark by rail in roughly 20–24 days total versus ~32–40 days by sea plus inland. The Fehmarn Belt tunnel (18 km, expected ~2029) will cut the Hamburg–Copenhagen rail journey from roughly 4.5 hours to about 2.5 hours, tightening rail\u2019s edge for Zealand cargo specifically.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Do I need an EORI to import into Denmark?',
    a: 'Yes — one EORI (Economic Operators Registration and Identification) number is required for any import into the EU, and it must exist before the goods arrive; zero Danish imports clear without it.',
  },
  {
    q: 'What is the difference between FCL and LCL for China to Denmark?',
    a: 'FCL is priced per container (northern-range benchmark 20ft $1,200–$4,800, 40ft $1,600–$6,800; Aarhus-specific figures are not published and must be requested) and LCL per cubic metre ($60–$160/CBM), with the planning break-even near 15 CBM — below that LCL is usually cheaper, above it FCL starts to win.',
  },
  {
    q: 'What documents are needed for Danish customs clearance?',
    a: 'The core set is four documents plus one classification — commercial invoice, bill of lading (or rail/CMR consignment note for rail cargo), packing list and the correct 10-digit TARIC code, backed by your EORI number. Regulated products add CE marking evidence and chemicals add REACH registration.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Can I defer VAT when importing into Denmark?',
    a: 'Denmark, like other EU states, allows EU customs warehousing (procedures 42/63) to defer import VAT until goods are released for free circulation — a working-capital lever worth more at 25% VAT than in a 19% or 20% market.',
  },
  {
    q: 'What are CE marking and REACH, and do they apply in Denmark?',
    a: 'CE marking is the EU conformity mark required for regulated products — electronics, toys, machinery and medical devices — and REACH is the EU chemical regulation requiring registration for chemical substances. Both apply in Denmark exactly as in every member state.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Do SABER or SASO apply to Denmark?',
    a: 'No — SABER and SASO are Saudi-only conformity systems and apply to zero Danish or EU imports. Denmark uses CE marking and REACH instead, so do not copy a Saudi SABER/SASO step into a Danish quote.',
  },
];

