        <p><strong>TL;DR:</strong> Denmark is Sweden&rsquo;s VAT twin but its currency opposite: both charge <strong>25% VAT</strong>, but Denmark&rsquo;s krone is pegged to the euro at <strong>±2.25%</strong> (ERM II) while Sweden&rsquo;s krona floats — so the statutory landed cost is identical (<strong>5% duty + 25% VAT = ×1.3125</strong>, a $10,000 CIF becomes <strong>$13,125</strong>), yet Denmark&rsquo;s currency risk is structurally bounded. The flat 25% has <strong>no reduced band</strong> (0% only on newspapers), so its effective VAT on food and books exceeds Sweden&rsquo;s. <strong>Aarhus (DKAAR)</strong> is the North Sea/Kattegat mainline gate handling <strong>~70% of Danish containers</strong>, while <strong>Copenhagen (DKCPH)</strong> is the Øresund/Baltic capital gate. Sea runs <strong>~32–40 days</strong> via the Cape, rail <strong>~20–24 days</strong> via Hamburg — and the Fehmarn Belt tunnel (~2029) will cut Hamburg–Copenhagen rail from <strong>~4.5h to ~2.5h</strong>.</p>
      </blockquote>
      <p class="small muted">
        Confidence badges separate verified figures from indicative planning notes. Denmark&rsquo;s FCL/LCL
        benchmarks come from the WorldFreightHub Europe route data Rotterdam northern-range corridor
