  <section class="section container">
    <h2>1. Why Denmark deserves its own China routing lens</h2>
    <p>
      Denmark is the Nordic market most freight guides flatten into &ldquo;a 25% VAT country,&rdquo; and that
      flattening hides the one fact that actually decides the landed cost: <strong>the currency regime</strong>.
      The country data shows the currency as <strong>DKK</strong> — not the euro — and a <strong>single 25%
      VAT rate</strong> with <strong>no reduced band</strong>, layered on top of the <strong>EU Common Customs
      Tariff</strong>, a duty line that is uniform across every member state and varies only by HS code,
      typically <strong>0–12% for consumer goods</strong> with no China–EU free-trade agreement in force.
    </p>
    <p>
      The statutory gate is the same as Sweden&rsquo;s — <strong>25% VAT</strong> — but the currency risk is not.
      Denmark&rsquo;s krone is <strong>pegged to the euro at ±2.25%</strong> under ERM II (1 EUR ≈ 7.46 DKK),
      which means the EUR→DKK leg of a landed-cost quote is fixed and only the USD→EUR leg floats. Sweden&rsquo;s
      krona, by contrast, floats freely against <em>both</em> — so a Swedish importer carries two unbounded legs
      where a Danish one carries one. On identical cargo the statutory landed cost is identical, but the
      currency risk is structurally different.
    </p>
    <p>
      Denmark changes the equation a second way: <strong>the flat 25% has no reduced band</strong>. Sweden
      applies 12% and 6% to food, books and hospitality; Denmark applies 0% only to newspapers and periodicals
      and 25% to everything else. So on a food or books consignment, Denmark&rsquo;s <em>effective</em> VAT is
      higher than Sweden&rsquo;s even though both headline at 25% — a subtlety a &ldquo;25%&rdquo; summary loses.
      On the commercial side, the honest position is that <strong>FCL and LCL ranges are published only as a
      northern-range Rotterdam reference</strong> (MEDIUM confidence), while Aarhus-specific, rail, air, express
      and DDP dollar figures are not — so those lines stay explicit request-for-quote markers instead of borrowed
      numbers.
    </p>
  </section>

