  <section class="section container">
    <h2>3. Transit times by origin port and mode</h2>
    <p>
      The China-to-Denmark clock has two beats for ocean cargo: the <strong>sea leg to Aarhus</strong>,
      then the <strong>inland leg to the final site</strong>. The WorldFreightHub Europe route data puts sea
      transit at roughly <strong>32–40 days via the Cape of Good Hope</strong> from Shanghai or
      Ningbo-Zhoushan to Aarhus, with <strong>~1–2 days</strong> of inland rail/truck beyond the port. Direct
      China calls to Aarhus are thinner than on the northern range, so transshipment via Rotterdam
      or Hamburg is common and adds a move. Rail from Xi&rsquo;an or Chengdu runs <strong>18–22 days</strong>
      to Hamburg and then continues to Denmark in a planning total of roughly <strong>20–24 days</strong>.
      Danish air transit is not published, so that line stays explicit instead of borrowing a neighbouring
      airport figure.
    </p>

