      <div class="note mt-2">
        <strong>Critical point — the rail premium does not pay for itself on financing alone:</strong>
        rail saves roughly 12–16 days (20–24 versus ~32–40 sea plus inland) at roughly 2–3× the sea rate.
        At an 8% cost of capital a $50,000 container costs ≈ $11/day in transit, so ~14 days is only about
        <strong>$154 of financing saved</strong> — which alone does not pay the rail premium. Rail is
        justified instead by <strong>stockout avoidance and market timing</strong> on high-value or
        seasonal cargo, and for <strong>Zealand/Copenhagen cargo specifically</strong> the Fehmarn Belt
        tunnel (~2029) will tighten rail&rsquo;s edge by cutting the Hamburg–Copenhagen rail leg. Air is the
        extreme-time option, with no verified Danish rate or transit published, so request that as an
        itemised quote rather than assuming a neighbouring airport figure.
      </div>

