  <section class="section container">
    <h2>Key takeaways</h2>
    <ol>
      <li>At a 5% duty assumption, a $10,000 CIF shipment clears at $13,125: $500 duty (5% × 10,000), $2,625 VAT on the $10,500 duty-inclusive base (25% × 10,500), a 31.25% uplift (1.05 × 1.25 = 1.3125) — identical to Sweden's statutory stack, but on a currency that is pegged, not floating.</li>
      <li>Denmark's flat 25% VAT has no reduced band (0% only on newspapers), so food and books pay the full 25% that Sweden taxes at reduced rates — and it equals 26.25% of CIF (25% × 1.05), adding $125 more than flat 25% on $10,000 CIF (2,625 − 2,500).</li>
      <li>Rail at 20–24 days via Hamburg saves 8–20 days versus the 32–40-day sea window (8 ÷ 40 = 20%; 20 ÷ 32 = 63%) at roughly 2–3× the sea rate — and the Fehmarn Belt tunnel (~2029) will cut the Hamburg–Copenhagen rail leg from ~4.5h to ~2.5h, shrinking the rail advantage further.</li>
      <li>The 15 CBM heuristic is fragile: at LCL $60–160/CBM, 15 CBM costs $900–2,400 (15 × 60 = 900; 15 × 160 = 2,400), already $1,200 above the $1,200 20ft FCL floor at the top of the LCL range — the true crossover sits at 7.5–20 CBM (1,200 ÷ 160 = 7.5; 1,200 ÷ 60 = 20).</li>
      <li>Aarhus handles ~70% of Danish containers while Copenhagen is the Øresund/Baltic capital gate, so the wrong gate choice adds an inland leg that erases the ×1.3125 statutory certainty — and the krone's ±2.25% ERM II peg to the euro caps the currency swing a floating krona (Sweden) cannot.</li>
    </ol>
  </section>

